    ],
    topics: ['Design + Engineering', 'The Tooling Habit', 'Open-Sourced by Default'],
  },
  {
    id:       'philosophy',
    category: 'PHILOSOPHY',
    title:    'How I Work',
    meta:     'Fix the root cause, in public',
    body: [
      'When a tool I\'m using has a gap, the default move is to patch around it locally. I\'d rather fix it at the source, publish the fix, and let every future project inherit it — slower in the moment, compounding over time. Mercury\'s findings log and the open issues on Triforge and Strata are the proof of that habit, not a claim about it.',
      'Clarity over cleverness is the rule that has saved the most time. A clever workaround nobody else can maintain is a liability. A clear fix, logged with a reproduction case and a root cause, is something the next person — or the next version of me — can actually build on.',
      'I\'d rather say a feature isn\'t built yet than imply it is. Uranus exists specifically to list the real gaps in my own tools honestly, instead of dressing them up as finished.',
    ],
    topics: ['Fix the Root Cause', 'Clarity Over Cleverness', 'Say What Isn\'t Done'],
  },
  {
    id:       'stack',
    category: 'STACK',
    title:    'Tools of the Trade',
    meta:     'Design + engineering stack',
    body: [
      'Frontend: TypeScript, React, Astro, Next.js. Three.js and GLSL for 3D and shader work — mostly compiled from Triforge node graphs rather than hand-written these days. GSAP for animation, Swiper for carousels.',
      'CSS: strata-css, my own JIT framework with cascade layers and arbitrary-value utilities — this is the only CSS layer running on this site. No Tailwind, no Bootstrap underneath it.',
      'Deployment and tooling: Vercel for live demos, npm for publishing packages, GitHub for everything. I choose tools that match the problem, including the ones I had to build myself because nothing else fit.',
    ],
    topics: ['Frontend', 'CSS', 'Deployment + Tooling'],
  },
